# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

int64_t croi_syscall(uint64_t number, uint64_t a0, uint64_t a1, uint64_t a2,
                     uint64_t a3, uint64_t a4);
int64_t croi_syscall6(uint64_t number, uint64_t a0, uint64_t a1, uint64_t a2,
                      uint64_t a3, uint64_t a4, uint64_t a5);
```

# DESCRIPTION

croi has 91 system calls.
Each is a number, `CROI_SYS_<NAME>` in `<croi/syscall.h>`, and up to six integer arguments.
`croi_syscall` makes a call of up to five arguments and `croi_syscall6` one of six.
The pages of this section give each call's arguments in the order the kernel reads them, as a declaration with the call's own name; the declarations are not C functions.
The few that have a C wrapper in the header say so.

The number goes in `rax` (amd64), `x16` (arm64) or `a7` (rv64).
The arguments go in the first six integer argument registers: `rdi`, `rsi`, `rdx`, `r10`, `r8`, `r9` on amd64, `x0` to `x5` on arm64, `a0` to `a5` on rv64.
The result comes back in `rax`, `x0` or `a0`.

A call that needs more values than registers packs two into one argument, written `a | b << 32`: the low 32 bits are the first and the high 32 bits the second.
A pointer argument is a user address.
An out-parameter is a pointer the kernel writes through.

## Results

The result is an `int64_t`.
Zero is success, or for a call that returns a count or a value, that count or value.
A negative result is a status:

| Status | Value | Means |
| `CROI_OK` | 0 | success |
| `CROI_ERR_INTERNAL` | -1 | a fault in the kernel |
| `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED` | -2 | not for this object, option or architecture |
| `CROI_ERR_NO_RESOURCES` | -3 | a fixed resource is used up |
| `CROI_ERR_NO_MEMORY` | -4 | the kernel is out of memory, or an account's limit is reached |
| `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS` | -10 | an argument is malformed, or an out-parameter is not writable |
| `CROI_ERR_BAD_HANDLE` | -11 | the handle is not in the caller's table |
| `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE` | -12 | the handle is of another type of object |
| `CROI_ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` | -14 | a size, offset or count is outside what the object holds |
| `CROI_ERR_BUFFER_TOO_SMALL` | -15 | the buffer is too small for the answer |
| `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` | -20 | the object is not in a state that allows the call |
| `CROI_ERR_TIMED_OUT` | -21 | the deadline passed |
| `CROI_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT` | -22 | there is nothing to read, or no room to write |
| `CROI_ERR_CANCELED` | -23 | the wait ended before it finished |
| `CROI_ERR_PEER_CLOSED` | -24 | the other end of the channel is gone |
| `CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND` | -25 | there is no such thing |
| `CROI_ERR_ALREADY_EXISTS` | -26 | there is one already |
| `CROI_ERR_ALREADY_BOUND` | -27 | it is bound to something already |
| `CROI_ERR_UNAVAILABLE` | -28 | not available now |
| `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED` | -30 | a right is missing, or the caller may not |
| `CROI_ERR_IO` | -40 | the device or pager failed |
| `CROI_ERR_IO_DATA_INTEGRITY` | -42 | the pager found the data corrupt |
| `CROI_ERR_FILE_BIG` | -53 | a pager reports the file too big |
| `CROI_ERR_NO_SPACE` | -54 | a pager reports no space |

The last three are statuses a pager may give a page it cannot supply; see pager(2).
A call number the kernel does not have fails with `CROI_ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED`.

## Handles and rights

A process refers to a kernel object by a handle, a 32-bit value in its own table.
Zero is never a handle.
A handle carries rights, and a call names the rights it needs of each handle it takes.
A handle not in the table is `CROI_ERR_BAD_HANDLE`; one of the wrong type of object is `CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE`; one without the right is `CROI_ERR_ACCESS_DENIED`.
The pages say which right each call needs.
A call that needs a resource takes a handle to a resource(2) of the right kind and range.

| Right | Bit | Right | Bit |
| `CROI_RIGHT_DUPLICATE` | 0 | `CROI_RIGHT_GET_POLICY` | 10 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_TRANSFER` | 1 | `CROI_RIGHT_SET_POLICY` | 11 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_READ` | 2 | `CROI_RIGHT_SIGNAL` | 12 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_WRITE` | 3 | `CROI_RIGHT_WAIT` | 14 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_EXECUTE` | 4 | `CROI_RIGHT_INSPECT` | 15 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_MAP` | 5 | `CROI_RIGHT_MANAGE_JOB` | 16 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_GET_PROPERTY` | 6 | `CROI_RIGHT_MANAGE_PROCESS` | 17 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_SET_PROPERTY` | 7 | `CROI_RIGHT_MANAGE_THREAD` | 18 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_ENUMERATE` | 8 | `CROI_RIGHT_APPLY_PROFILE` | 19 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_DESTROY` | 9 | `CROI_RIGHT_OP_CHILDREN` | 21 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_ATTACH_VMO` | 23 | `CROI_RIGHT_MANAGE_VMO` | 24 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_RESIZE` | 22 | `CROI_RIGHT_CHARGE` | 25 |
| `CROI_RIGHT_SAME_RIGHTS` | 31 | | |

`CROI_RIGHTS_BASIC` is TRANSFER, DUPLICATE, WAIT and INSPECT.
`CROI_RIGHT_SAME_RIGHTS` is not a right: handle_duplicate(2) reads it as "the rights the handle has".

The type of an object is one of:

| Type | Value | Type | Value |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_PROCESS` | 1 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_RESOURCE` | 15 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_THREAD` | 2 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_JOB` | 17 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_VMO` | 3 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_VMAR` | 18 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_CHANNEL` | 4 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_IOMMU` | 23 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_PORT` | 6 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_BTI` | 24 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_INTERRUPT` | 9 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_PROFILE` | 25 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_LOG` | 12 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_PMT` | 26 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_SUSPEND_TOKEN` | 27 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_PAGER` | 28 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_EXCEPTION` | 29 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_MSI` | 32 |
| `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_COUNTER` | 34 | `CROI_OBJ_TYPE_MEMORY_ACCOUNT` | 49 |

Every object has a koid, a 64-bit number unique for the life of the system, that object_get_info(2) reports.

## Batches

A call that is often made many times takes an array of items and a count, and does the whole array in one trap.
handle_duplicate, object_bind, port_queue, port_wait, channel_write, channel_read, counter_add, interrupt_ack, vmar_map, vmar_unmap and vmar_protect work this way.
Each item has an `int32_t status` field.
A count of 0, or one past the call's limit (64 items for every one of them), or an array the kernel cannot read or write, fails the whole call with a negative status before any item is done.
Otherwise the call returns how many items succeeded and writes each processed item's status back.
Some calls stop at the first item that fails, and the later items are not touched; the others go on past a failure.
Each page says which.
A caller that wants to know why an item failed reads its `status`.

## User memory

The kernel reads and writes user memory only through checked copies.
A pointer into memory that is not mapped, or not writable for an out-parameter, makes the call fail with `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`; it never crashes the kernel.
A call checks every out-parameter before it changes anything, so a call that fails for a bad pointer has not created a handle or moved a message.

## Deadlines

A deadline is an absolute time on the monotonic clock in nanoseconds, the clock the vDSO reads.
`UINT64_MAX` is no deadline.
A wait never returns early for its deadline.

## Policy

A job's policy can deny or kill on the calls that create things (vmo_create, channel_create, port_create, process_create, pager_create) and on use of a bad handle.
See job(2).

